<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Idrisid Emirate of Asir</h1><p class="st-meta">1906 CE – 1934 CE · capital Sabya</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Sufi-led emirate on the Red Sea coast of Arabia that rebelled against the Ottomans, allied with Italy and Britain, and was absorbed by Saudi Arabia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-ali-al-idrisi">Muhammad ibn Ali al-Idrisi</a>, great-grandson of the Moroccan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> teacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-idris">Ahmad ibn Idris</a>, settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabya">Sabya</a> in the coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tihama">Tihama</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asir">Asir</a>. Building on his religious prestige, he rallied the tribes against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yemen-vilayet">Yemen Vilayet</a> around 1906-1910 and established an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Idrisi ruled as a religious reformer and tribal leader, enforcing Islamic law and suppressing feuds. During the Italo-Turkish War <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> supplied him with arms, and in 1915 he signed a treaty with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. After the war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> handed him the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hodeidah">Hodeidah</a> in 1921, putting him at odds with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-muhammad-hamid-ed-din">Imam Yahya's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mutawakkilite-kingdom-of-yemen">Mutawakkilite Kingdom of Yemen</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Muhammad's death in 1923, succession quarrels weakened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> and Yahya took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hodeidah">Hodeidah</a>. In 1926 Hasan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi">al-Idrisi</a> accepted a Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a> annexed the territory in 1930, and after a failed revolt and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saudi-yemeni-war">Saudi-Yemeni war</a> of 1934 it was absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/idrisid-emirate-of-asir.jpg" alt="Idrisid Emirate of Asir" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: Abo Yemen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Emirate_of_Asir.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1906 CE – 1934 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sabya</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad ibn Ali al-Idrisi, Hasan ibn Ali al-Idrisi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (Idrisi Sufi order)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>, <a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Idrisi Emirate, Emirate of Sabya, Idrisid State</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1920&amp;focus=idrisid-emirate-of-asir" aria-label="Idrisid Emirate of Asir on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5839 1541 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Idrisid Emirate of Asir lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6159 1797L5709 970M6159 1797L4997 651M6159 1797L6241 1527M6159 1797L6241 1527"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M4997 651h0M6241 1527h0M6241 1527h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6159 1797h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6159 1797h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sabya. <a href="/a